Story: Planning a hand-launched circuit
  As a pilot at the field
  I want to plan a takeoff, a few waypoints and a return home
  So that the plane flies the circuit on its own after I throw it

  A circuit is the simplest useful mission. The plane takes off, flies to some waypoints
  and comes back. The takeoff waits for your throw. The return brings the plane back to the
  point where you armed it.

  Scenario: Takeoff, three waypoints, home
    When the operator adds a takeoff
    Then the plan has 1 item
    And the takeoff is planned
    When the operator plans three waypoints
    Then the plan has 4 items
    When the operator adds a return home
    Then the last plan item is RTL
    When the operator uploads the plan
    Then the vehicle mission is up to date

Story: Fixing a misplaced waypoint
  As a pilot
  I want to take back a waypoint I put in the wrong place
  So that I don't have to start the plan again

  It is easy to remove a waypoint that you put in the wrong place. A right-click removes the
  last item. Then you can click the correct place.

  Scenario: Two clicks and a right-click
    When the operator plans two waypoints
    Then the plan has 2 items
    When the operator right-clicks the map
    Then the plan has 1 item

Story: Clearing the ridge
  As a pilot planning over the hills
  I want to plan higher and check the plan against the terrain
  So that no waypoint flies the plane into the ground

  The ridges of the valley are higher than the default [[plan altitude]]. Before you put
  waypoints near the ridges, increase the plan altitude. Then compare the plan with the
  terrain. The planner raises each waypoint that is too low.

  Scenario: Raise the plan, add a waypoint, check it
    When the operator raises the plan altitude
    When the operator raises the plan altitude
    Then the plan altitude is 60 m
    When the operator clicks the map
    Then the newest waypoint is at the plan altitude
    When the operator checks the heights against the terrain
    Then the waypoints are clear of the terrain

Story: Keeping a mission for the field
  As a pilot
  I want to save a mission and load it again later
  So that I can fly the same circuit next time

  Save a plan that you want to use again. If you change the plan after that, load the file
  to get the saved plan back.

  Scenario: Save, change, load
    When the operator plans two waypoints
    When the operator saves the plan to a file
    Then the mission file is up to date
    When the operator clicks the map
    Then the mission file is out of date
    When the operator loads the plan from the file
    Then the mission file is up to date

Story: Surveying a field
  As a pilot mapping a field
  I want the planner to lay out the survey lines for me
  So that the photos cover the whole field

  To make a map of a field, draw a line around the field. The planner fills the area with
  survey lines. Then write the plan to the plane, as with all other plans.

  Scenario: Draw a grid and upload it
    When the operator draws a survey grid
    Then the plan has at least 4 items
    When the operator uploads the plan
    Then the vehicle mission is up to date

Story: Planning at the altitude limit
  As a pilot planning over the mountains
  I want the plan altitude to stop at its limit
  So that I can't plan the plane higher than it should fly

  The plan altitude cannot go higher than 300 m above home. At 300 m, ALT+ does nothing, if
  you press it one time or many times.

  Scenario: ALT+ from 290 m
    Given the plan altitude is 290 m
    When the operator raises the plan altitude
    Then the plan altitude is 300 m
    When the operator raises the plan altitude
    Then the plan altitude is 300 m
